---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro"
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro"
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro"
import AddToCalendar from "../../components/AddToCalendarLink.astro"
import ExpandableBio from "../../components/util/ExpandableBio.astro"

import { Image } from "astro:assets"
import photoMeyer from "../../assets/speakers/2026-10-22-portrait-meyer.jpg"
import photoHourigan from "../../assets/speakers/2026-10-22-portrait-hourigan.jpg"
import photoPhilp from "../../assets/speakers/2026-10-22-portrait-philp.jpg"
import photoObrien from "../../assets/speakers/2026-10-22-portrait-obrien.jpg"
import calendarIcon22 from "../../assets/icons/calendar-icon-22.svg"
let icsUrl = "/media/2026-10-22/Professionals-Platform-English.ics"
---

<Layout>
    <Header />
    <article class="mx-auto max-w-[1024px] p-4">
        <div class="mb-4">
            <ul class="tabs">
                <li class="tab">
                    <a href="/sessions/2026-07">23 July 2026</a>
                </li>
                <li class="tab selected">
                    <span data-href="/sessions/2026-10">22 October 2026</span>
                </li>
            </ul>
            <h1 class="my-4 text-2xl">
                What Leadership Role Can the Legal Community Play in Addressing
                Alcohol Use Disorder (AUD)?
            </h1>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-4">
            <div class="prose col-span-2 rounded-lg border-gray-300 py-2">
                <p>
                    Join judges, lawyers, general counsel, lawyer assistance
                    professionals, and leaders from across the legal community
                    to explore how judicial leadership, research, and lawyer
                    assistance programs are strengthening understanding of
                    Alcohol Use Disorder (AUD), enhancing professional
                    well-being, and improving outcomes for the individuals,
                    families, and communities served by the legal system.
                </p>
                <p>Who Should Attend:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Judges and members of the judiciary</li>
                    <li>Lawyers in private and public practice</li>
                    <li>General Counsel and in-house legal departments</li>
                    <li>Lawyer Assistance Program (LAP) professionals</li>
                    <li>Bar association and legal organization leaders</li>
                    <li>Legal aid and public interest organizations</li>
                    <li>Law students and legal educators</li>
                    <li>Healthcare and behavioral health professionals</li>
                    <li>Recovery and community leaders</li>
                    <li>Anyone interested in lawyer well-being and recovery</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div
                class="from-powderblue-100 to-powderblue-050 col-span-1 rounded-lg bg-gradient-to-b px-4 py-2">
                <div>
                    <strong>Thursday 22 October 2026</strong>
                    <ul class="mt-1">
                        <li>7:00–8:30 PM ET</li>
                        <li>6:00–7:30 PM CT</li>
                        <li>4:00–5:30 PM PT</li>
                        <li class="mt-2">
                            <a
                                href={icsUrl}
                                class="hover:text-powderblue-700 inline-flex items-center gap-2">
                                <Image
                                    src={calendarIcon22}
                                    width="16"
                                    height="16"
                                    alt=""
                                />
                                <span>Add to Calendar</span>
                            </a>
                            <!-- <AddToCalendar date={"2026-07-23"} /> -->
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="mt-4">
                    <strong>Virtual Access (Zoom)</strong>
                    <div class="mt-1">
                        <p>Meeting ID:</p>
                        <p>867 7265 7920</p>
                        <p class="mt-2">Passcode:</p>
                        <p>853223</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="mt-4">
                    Spanish and French interpretation available
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h2 class="my-4 text-2xl font-bold">About the Speakers</h2>
        <section class="bios grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
            <div
                class="card speaker-card bg-zest-100 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-gray-200">
                <div class="speaker-info">
                    <Image
                        src={photoMeyer}
                        alt="Justice Chief Judge Kerry Meyer"
                        class="speaker-photo"
                    />
                    <div class="px-3 pt-2">
                        <h4 class="mt-2 mb-2 font-bold">
                            <span class="speaker-name text-xl">
                                Photo of Chief Judge Kerry Meyer
                            </span>
                            <span>Minneapolis</span>
                        </h4>
                        <div class="text-sm">
                            <p class="mt-2">
                                Class A Trustee, General Service Board of
                                Alcoholics Anonymous
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <hr class="border-zest-400 my-4" />
                <ExpandableBio class="rounded-lg px-3 pb-3 text-sm">
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        Kerry Meyer is the current Chief Judge of the trial
                        court in Minneapolis (Hennepin County) Minnesota. She is
                        also a non-alcoholic trustee on the General Service
                        Board of Alcoholics Anonymous. Judge Meyer has presided
                        over many types of cases in her 20 years as a judge,
                        including over three years as a fulltime Treatment Court
                        judge.
                    </p>
                </ExpandableBio>
            </div>

            <div
                class="card speaker-card bg-zest-100 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-gray-200">
                <div class="speaker-info">
                    <Image
                        src={photoHourigan}
                        alt="Yvette Hourigan, Esq."
                        class="speaker-photo"
                    />
                    <div class="px-3 pt-2">
                        <h4 class="mt-2 mb-2 font-bold">
                            <span class="speaker-name text-xl">
                                Yvette Hourigan, Esq.
                            </span>
                        </h4>
                        <div class="text-sm">
                            <p>
                                Chair, Commission on Lawyer Assistance Programs
                                (CoLAP), American Bar Association
                            </p>
                            <p class="mt-2">
                                Director, Kentucky Lawyer Assistance Program
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <hr class="border-zest-400 my-4" />
                <ExpandableBio class="rounded-lg px-3 pb-3 text-sm">
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        Yvette Hourigan is the director of the Kentucky Lawyer
                        Assistance Program (KYLAP). Yvette graduated from Murray
                        State University and the J. David Rosenberg College of
                        Law at the University of Kentucky. She is a licensed
                        attorney, a Certified Employee Assistance Professional,
                        and a Certified Peer Support Specialist. She is
                        certified in Mental Health First Aid and QPR Question
                        Persuade Refer Suicide Prevention.
                    </p>

                    <p class="mt-2">
                        After law school, Yvette practiced in Central and
                        Eastern Kentucky as a civil trial lawyer focusing on
                        plaintiff’s personal injury and domestic litigation. She
                        was appointed KYLAP Executive Director by the Kentucky
                        Supreme Court in 2011. She is the Chair of the American
                        Bar Association’s Commission on Lawyer Assistance
                        Programs (COLAP); the former chair of the ABA/COLAP
                        Diversity, Equity & Inclusion Committee; and former
                        member of the National Task Force on Lawyer Well-Being.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        Yvette is the 2025 recipient of the KBA Young Lawyers
                        Division Outstanding Service to Young Lawyers Award. She
                        is the recipient of the Algernon Sydney Sullivan
                        Medallion, a humanitarian award, by the University of
                        Kentucky. She was awarded the Kentucky Supreme Court
                        Chief Justice’s Special Service Award in 2024. In 2021
                        she was awarded the KBA President’s Special Service
                        Award by President Tom Kerrick for KYLAP’s service to
                        Kentucky’s legal community during the COVID-19 pandemic.
                        She also received the Dave Nee Foundation’s “Uncommon
                        Counselor” Award for her work in suicide prevention in
                        the legal profession as well as the Kentucky Justice
                        Association and Family Safety Foundation’s Consumer
                        Safety Award. This honor goes to an individual who is an
                        outspoken advocate for consumer rights or who has risked
                        their own well-being for the future health or safety of
                        others.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        She speaks locally and nationally on topics impacting
                        lawyer competence and well-being, impairment, addiction,
                        and suicide prevention, and shares her personal story of
                        recovery from substance use disorder. KYLAP’s work on
                        lawyer mental health, well-being and suicide prevention
                        has been featured nationally on Legal View with Ashleigh
                        Banfield, CNN, Bloomberg Law, The Guardian, NPR, and in
                        The Huffington Post.
                    </p>
                </ExpandableBio>
            </div>

            <div
                class="card speaker-card bg-zest-100 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-gray-200">
                <div class="speaker-info">
                    <Image
                        src={photoPhilp}
                        alt="Justice Robert Philp"
                        class="speaker-photo"
                    />
                    <div class="px-3 pt-2">
                        <h4 class="mt-2 mb-2 font-bold">
                            <span class="speaker-name text-xl">
                                Justice Robert A. Philp
                            </span>
                            <span class="block">King’s Counsel</span>
                        </h4>
                        <div class="text-sm">
                            <p>Partner, Twinn & Philp</p>
                            <p class="mt-2">
                                Co-founder and Former Director, Alberta Lawyers'
                                Assistance Society
                            </p>
                            <p class="mt-2">
                                Former Chief, Alberta Human Rights Commission
                                and Tribunals
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <hr class="border-zest-400 my-4" />
                <ExpandableBio class="rounded-lg px-3 pb-3 text-sm">
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        Justice Robert A. Philp, King’s Counsel, is a partner
                        with Twinn & Philp in Edmonton, Alberta, and a respected
                        Canadian jurist, lawyer, and human rights leader with
                        decades of experience in the justice system, public
                        service, and legal profession. Throughout his career, he
                        has combined legal practice, judicial leadership, and
                        public service with a deep commitment to justice,
                        equity, dignity, access to justice, and lawyer
                        well-being.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        Justice Philp served as Chief of the Alberta Human
                        Rights Commission and Tribunals, where he was
                        responsible for the Commission's administrative,
                        operational, and adjudicative functions, overseeing
                        complex human rights appeals and organizational
                        leadership. He also served as a Provincial Court Judge
                        in Alberta and as Deputy Judge for the Northwest
                        Territories, presiding over criminal trials, complex
                        hearings, judicial case management, and bail
                        proceedings. Earlier in his career, he practiced labour,
                        employment, human rights, and administrative law,
                        appearing before all levels of court, including the
                        Supreme Court of Canada.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        In addition to his legal practice and judicial service,
                        Justice Philp has held numerous leadership appointments
                        within the legal profession and public service. He has
                        served as Director of the Lawyers' Assistance Program,
                        Bencher of the Law Society of Alberta, Adjunct Professor
                        at the University of Alberta, and guest lecturer on
                        human rights, criminal law, administrative law, and
                        employment law. He has also devoted many years to
                        mentoring law students and early-career lawyers while
                        serving on numerous nonprofit and community boards.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        Justice Philp's distinguished public service has been
                        recognized through numerous honours, including the
                        Distinguished Service Award from the Law Society of
                        Alberta and the Canadian Bar Association, the Alberta
                        Centennial Medal, the King's Coronation Medal, the
                        Queen's Golden Jubilee Medal, the Community Service
                        Award from Native Counselling Services of Alberta, and
                        the Distinguished Alumni Award from the University of
                        Alberta.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        He continues to serve as a leader, mentor, and advocate
                        whose work reflects a lifelong commitment to justice,
                        public service, and strengthening the legal profession.
                    </p>
                </ExpandableBio>
            </div>
        </section>

        <h2 class="my-4 text-2xl font-bold">Featured Commentary</h2>
        <section class="bios grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
            <div
                class="card speaker-card bg-zest-100 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-gray-200">
                <div class="speaker-info">
                    <Image
                        src={photoObrien}
                        alt="Justice Mary K. O’Brien"
                        class="speaker-photo"
                    />
                    <div class="px-3 pt-2">
                        <h4 class="mt-2 mb-2 font-bold">
                            <span class="speaker-name text-xl">
                                Justice Mary K. O’Brien
                            </span>
                        </h4>
                        <div class="text-sm">
                            <p class="mt-2">
                                Illinois Supreme Court, Third District
                            </p>
                            <p class="mt-2">
                                Emeritus Board Member, Illinois Lawyers’
                                Assistance Program
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <hr class="border-zest-400 my-4" />
                <ExpandableBio class="rounded-lg px-3 pb-3 text-sm">
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        Justice Mary Kay O’Brien has been a judge for over 20
                        years, first on the Third District Appellate Court and
                        most recently as a Justice on the Illinois Supreme
                        Court, but her commitment to public service began long
                        before her judicial career. As a student at Western
                        Illinois University, O’Brien was elected to represent
                        students on the Student Government Association and
                        advocated for programming on the Committee on Student
                        Activity Fees. Upon graduation, O’Brien worked for the
                        Illinois Attorney General in the Kankakee Regional
                        Office before entering the University of Illinois
                        College of Law in August 1991.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        As a new lawyer, Justice O’Brien fine-tuned her advocacy
                        skills, helping clients navigate the court system and in
                        1996 she broadened the reach of her advocacy skills by
                        winning a seat in the Illinois General Assembly. As a
                        state representative, O’Brien worked tirelessly on
                        behalf of working families and crime victims, working to
                        increase the quality and availability of daycare, fought
                        to expand the Family and Medical Leave Act as well as
                        fighting to increase protection for victims of violent
                        crimes. O’Brien chaired the Judiciary II Committee on
                        Criminal Law, reforming the state’s death penalty
                        statutes, championed reforms in the Grain Insurance
                        Code, opened access to generic drugs for Illinois
                        consumers and served as a taxpayer watchdog on the
                        Illinois Audit Commission. Those experiences led O’Brien
                        to seek a seat on the appellate court where her mission
                        was to provide a level playing field for all parties to
                        a dispute.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        On the appellate court, O’Brien served on numerous
                        committees, including the Supreme Court Rules,
                        Legislative, and Appellate Court Access to Justice
                        Committees, the Illinois Courts’ Commission and the
                        Lawyers Assistance Program. As a member of the Illinois
                        Supreme Court, Justice O’Brien serves as the Court’s
                        Liaison to the Supreme Court Rules Committee, the
                        Supreme Court Committee on Juvenile Courts, the Lawyers
                        Trust Fund and the Lawyers Assistance Program. O’Brien
                        continues to work to increase her understanding of the
                        law and has helped teach continuing legal education
                        classes. She is active with several bar associations,
                        including the Illinois State Bar Association, the
                        Illinois Judges’ Association, the Women’s Bar
                        Association of Illinois, the DuPage County Bar
                        Association, the Will County Bar Association, and the
                        Black Bar Association of Will County. Justice O’Brien
                        was recently honored by the Illinois Judges Foundation
                        with its Distinguished Service Award, as well as
                        receiving the Illinois Bar Foundation’s Champion Award,
                        and the Ruth Bader Ginsburg Award for Judicial
                        Achievement Award from the Women’s Bar Association of
                        Illinois.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-2">
                        Justice O’Brien continues to be involved in the local
                        community by assisting with the high school mock trial
                        programs at Joliet West and Joliet Central High Schools,
                        volunteering with the Mission Star Mission Shelter and
                        recently with My Aging Services a non-profit aimed at
                        assisting families with adult children with physical and
                        developmental disabilities.
                    </p>
                </ExpandableBio>
            </div>
        </section>
    </article>
    <Footer />

    <style>
        .speaker-card {
            container-type: inline-size;
        }

        .speaker-info {
            /* condensed (stacked) rules go here */
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
            .speaker-name {
                display: block;
            }

            /* spread-out rules go here */
            @container (min-width: 48ch) {
                flex-direction: row;
                .speaker-photo {
                    max-width: 33%;
                }
            }
        }

        hr {
            @container (min-width: 48ch) {
                display: none;
            }
        }
    </style>
</Layout>
